  "body": "Found in the review read of PR #3668 (the catalog case `3d/rough-finish`, #2866). Read from the posted program; no machine run. Refs #2392.\n\n**What the program does:** the entry of the operation is a helix, and each level starts with a helix. But in a level the tool goes in again about 29 times with a straight feed plunge from the clearance height. Each of these goes into a row where only about half of the tool width is cleared, and the full stepdown of 2 mm is stock.\n\n**Example** (lines 126-128): `G00 X8.081 Y-9.48 Z47.` / `G00 X-8.072 Y-9.48 Z47.` / `G01 X-8.072 Y-9.48 Z16. F300`. The row at Y-12.48 cleared up to Y-9.48; the band from Y-9.48 to Y-6.48 has stock from Z18 to Z16. The same at line 1634 (`G01 X-7.199 Y-21.48 Z8. F300`).\n\n**Expected:** a re-entry uses the entry type of the operation (a helix or a ramp), or it goes down only where the full tool disc is in cleared stock. A tool that does not cut at its centre must not get a straight plunge into stock; check that the centre-cut law judges these moves.\n\n**Also:** each entry feeds the full 39 mm from Z47 at the plunge feed, and the roughing has 16 moves of zero length.\n\n**Done when:** a test on this case finds no feed move that goes down into uncut stock outside a helix or a ramp. The catalog program is then made again.\n",
